/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

  /* Control. Must come back at exactly 100. */
  create_object FORAGE
  {
    number_of_objects 100
    set_gaia_object_only
  }

  /* minus seven mod two. 90 truncated, 110 floored. */
  create_object OLIVE_TREE
  {
    number_of_objects (0 - 7 % 2 * 10 + 100)
    set_gaia_object_only
  }

  /* seven mod minus two. 110 truncated, 90 floored. */
  create_object GOLD
  {
    number_of_objects (7 % -2 * 10 + 100)
    set_gaia_object_only
  }

  /* minus seven mod minus two. 90 under both sign models, 110 only if the
     engine always returns a positive remainder. */
  create_object STONE
  {
    number_of_objects (0 - 7 % -2 * 10 + 100)
    set_gaia_object_only
  }

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_47 - which way does the modulo operator round for negative operands?
Feeds: preview-design Sec.3.6, src/preview/generator/mathEval.ts,
       parser-design Sec.13 item 18, Sec.15 item 19

QUESTION
The math evaluator implements truncation toward zero for the remainder operator,
and the spec now states that as the default rather than as a measurement. It was
inherited from a parser-design question that has been phrased as open across
several revisions with no answer anywhere in the build log. The preview's own
spec previously told implementers to "implement whatever the in-game test
showed", which under a do-not-deviate banner points at nothing at all.

The two candidate semantics disagree whenever exactly one operand is negative:

                        truncated    floored
  minus seven mod two      -1           1
  seven mod minus two       1          -1
  minus seven mod minus two -1         -1

The third row agrees under both, which is why it is here: it separates a genuine
sign rule from an "always return a positive remainder" implementation, which
would give 1 on all three.

METHOD
Three object commands whose counts are computed rather than written. Each
expression multiplies the remainder by ten and adds a hundred, so the two models
land 20 apart instead of 2 apart and a single failed placement cannot flip the
reading.

  expression                       truncated   floored   always-positive
  (0 - 7 % 2 * 10 + 100)               90        110          110
  (7 % -2 * 10 + 100)                 110         90          110
  (0 - 7 % -2 * 10 + 100)              90         90          110

Note the arithmetic is strictly LEFT TO RIGHT with no operator precedence, which
is the language's own documented rule and is what makes these expressions read
as intended. (0 - 7 % 2 * 10 + 100) evaluates as (((( 0 - 7 ) % 2 ) * 10 ) + 100),
not as the schoolbook grouping.

That rule is also why the first negative is written as a subtraction from zero
and the second is written as a literal. Reaching the second operand by
subtraction does not work: in (7 % 0 - 2 ...) the remainder is taken against
zero and evaluated before the subtraction ever happens, so the expression tests
division by zero - a rule this project has already traced through the guide's
own worked examples - instead of a negative divisor. A leading minus on a
literal is ordinary in this language (negative clumping factors and circle radii
are both live in the corpus), so the literal form is the safe one here and the
zero-subtraction form is the safe one for the left operand.

The fourth command is a fixed control at 100. If it does not come back at 100
the map is too crowded to place reliably and the other three counts are floors
rather than measurements - in that case rerun on a larger map before reading.

WHAT TO DO WITH THE ANSWER
Truncated confirms the shipped default and the verify marker comes off both
Sec.3.6 and the parser-design item. Floored means mathEval.ts is wrong for every
expression with a negative operand, and its worked-example tests need
re-deriving rather than patching - the guide's own examples were reproduced
exactly under the truncated model, so a floored answer means those examples were
being read wrongly too.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count. ONE RUN. The expressions are
deterministic; only placement is random, and the control guards that.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>"
  The default histogram is the whole reading. Four counts, three of which are
  the answer.
*/
